  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is drospirenone, a rigid lipophilic 17-spirolactone steroid; the closest steroid-context record, UDCA, reports 78–87% systemic availability after oral dosing, well above the 20% threshold.",
      "card_ids": ["C17"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Multiple human absolute-bioavailability reference contexts are all well above 20%: vildagliptin ~84–85%, fenspiride 90%, sulbactam 70–80%, hexobarbital 90%, thalidomide 80–100%, anagliptin >73%, and pentazocine 0.68–0.70 in cirrhosis.",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C14", "C15", "C04", "C05", "C13", "C18", "C22", "C12", "C20", "C09", "C16"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Lipophilic neutral rigid scaffolds are represented by aminoglutethimide and pyridoglutethimide at ~100% F, supporting transfer to the query's lipophilic neutral rigid scaffold.",
      "card_ids": ["C10", "C06"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L2 adds high-F context: vildagliptin >90% in monkeys and 100% in dogs, aminoglutethimide >95% and tablet/solution equivalence, pentazocine 68% in cirrhosis, and anagliptin 100% in dogs.",
      "card_ids": ["C25", "C29", "C47", "C49", "C34", "C41", "C48", "C23", "C54", "C26", "C28", "C44"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The only sub-20% value in the visible record is hexobarbital rat F=12% (C42); it is species-specific and not transferable to the query. Anagliptin rat values (23–54%) are at or above threshold, and sulbactam's 'limited' statement is a generic absorption comment, not affirmative F<20% evidence for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C42", "C24", "C31", "C40", "C50", "C55", "C53"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral-exposure records for canrenone (M01), the closest structural analog with the same 17-spirolactone/3-keto steroid core, show substantial dose-dependent oral AUC in humans (e.g., AUC0-24 4149 ng·hr/ml after 200 mg; C68), demonstrating that this scaffold is orally absorbed.",
      "card_ids": ["C68", "C56", "C70", "C82"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 records for drospirenone-containing oral contraceptives (EE 0.03 mg/drospirenone 3 mg) confirm oral tablet administration and systemic exposure of the co-administered EE, but do not measure drospirenone itself; they are weak supportive context, not direct F evidence.",
      "card_ids": ["C60", "C75", "C92", "C94", "C101", "C102"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "oral_auc_cmax_exposure",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C68", "C56", "C70", "C82", "C60", "C75", "C92", "C94", "C101", "C102"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct drospirenone-specific absolute oral bioavailability record is present in L1–L3; all F evidence comes from reference contexts.",
    "L3 oral AUC/Cmax records lack IV comparators, so they cannot quantify F; canrenone is a metabolite of spironolactone, not the query parent.",
    "Records for drospirenone-containing oral contraceptives measure ethinylestradiol, not drospirenone.",
    "Query species, formulation, dose, prandial state, and disease condition are unspecified; species-dependent F values (e.g., hexobarbital human 90% vs rat 12%) limit certainty.",
    "The unfavorable solubility prior is not affirmative evidence of low F; L3 shows lipophilic steroids can achieve oral exposure."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "All threshold-relevant evidence, including the steroid-context UDCA (78–87%), multiple human absolute-F contexts, and L3 oral-exposure records for the closely related canrenone scaffold, supports F≥20%; no affirmative sub-20% evidence applies to the query. Prediction remains high with moderate confidence."
}

